Automated Cloud Infrastructure & CI/CD Pipeline
An end-to-end DevOps project featuring Infrastructure as Code (IaC), Containerization, and Automated SSL Deployment.

🚀 Project Overview
This project demonstrates a production-grade deployment workflow for web applications. While the application is a Python-based microservice, the core focus is the DevOps lifecycle: automating the provisioning of AWS resources, containerizing the application, and establishing a secure, "zero-touch" deployment pipeline.

🛠️ Tech Stack
Infrastructure: Terraform (AWS EC2, Security Groups)

Pipeline: GitLab CI/CD

Containerization: Docker & Docker Hub

Web Server: Nginx (Reverse Proxy & SSL Termination)

Security: OpenSSL (Self-signed certificates), SSH Key Management

Environment: Amazon Linux 2023 (AL2023)

🏗️ Architecture & Features
Infrastructure as Code: Uses Terraform to ensure the server environment is reproducible and version-controlled.

Multi-Stage Pipeline: Includes automated testing, Docker image building, and remote deployment stages.

Hardened Nginx Configuration: Features a custom "Canonical Reset" logic to prevent configuration drift and ensure a valid server state.

SSL Termination: Automates the generation and configuration of SSL certificates to secure traffic on Port 443.

Docker Orchestration: Implements automated pull-and-restart logic to ensure zero downtime during updates.

🔧 Engineering Challenges & Solutions
1. The "1-Byte Config" & Variable Injection
Challenge: Passing GitLab CI variables into a remote SSH session often resulted in empty strings or shell expansion errors, leading to broken Nginx configurations.
Solution: Implemented a "Hybrid Injection" method using quoted Heredocs (<< 'EOF'). By passing variables directly through the SSH command string and protecting the inner script from local expansion, I ensured that critical Nginx variables like $host were preserved while project variables like $IMAGE_NAME were correctly populated.

2. Nginx Configuration Resilience
Challenge: Standard sed edits on nginx.conf frequently caused syntax errors (e.g., location directive not allowed here).
Solution: Designed a "Canonical Reset" script that overwrites the master Nginx configuration with a known-good template during every deployment. This guarantees a clean state and eliminates manual troubleshooting on the server.

📖 How to Use
Provision: Run terraform apply to create the AWS environment.

Configure: Add the EC2_HOST, SSH_PRIVATE_KEY, and DOCKER_PASS to GitLab CI/CD variables.

Deploy: Push code to the main branch to trigger the automated build and deployment.